Found it. The attraction is **La Bufadora**, a natural blowhole/marine geyser located at the southern tip of the **Punta Banda peninsula, just south of Ensenada, Baja California, Mexico** (coordinates ~31.724, -116.723).

When ocean waves and air are forced into an underwater cave at the base of the cliff, the water is expelled upward in a roaring spout that can reach roughly 30–100 feet (about 10–30 m) high. It's widely described as the second-largest marine geyser in the world (the largest being in Oahu, Hawaii), and it's best viewed during high tide when the waves are strongest.

